deep.reconstruction:
Context: dated 5 June 1986, two years after the Esquisse d'un Programme (1984), whose §5–6 called for a 'topologie modérée' (tame topology) and a combinatorial theory of 'formes'. The heading announces an approach to a construction by recursion on the natural number n of a 'geometry of forms of dimension n'. This page sets up the first two steps axiomatically and purely combinatorially, with no point-set topology.

Dimension 0. A form of dimension 0 is a discrete set. Its elements are called 'lieux' (places, loci).

Dimension 1. The word 'Forme' is crossed out and replaced by 'Modèle', so what follows is a model or presentation of a form rather than the form itself. A model consists of:
(1) a set L of lieux;
(2) a subset L0 ⊂ L of 'critical' or 'branching' lieux;
(3) a symmetric relation R ⊂ L×L, 'x and y are disjoint', which implies 'x and y are distinct';
(4) a finer symmetric relation, 'x and y are adjacent';
(5) a still finer relation, 'x is a specialization of y', which forces x critical and y non-critical;
(6) a set S of 'segments';
(7) for each segment I, a two-element boundary δI ⊂ L of adjacent lieux, called its extremities;
(8) a subset Ĩ ⊂ L containing δI, the lieux 'on' the segment.

On the next page Grothendieck adds remarks and axioms:
- Adjacency is recoverable: x and y are adjacent iff δI = {x,y} for some segment I. So datum (4) is redundant, and the extremities of a segment must be disjoint.
- He conjectures that at most two segments share a given boundary {x,y}, with exactly two iff x,y lie on a connected component that is a circle.
- He asks whether a segment is determined by (δI, Ĩ), i.e. whether one may take S ⊂ 𝔓₂(L)×𝔓(L).
- Axiom E1: the relation 'x and y are not disjoint' is an equivalence relation. Its classes are the points. Several distinct critical lieux can define the same critical point.

Interpretation. A lieu is not a point but something like a local branch or germ of the form, in the way places of a curve sit over its singular points. A branch point carries several critical lieux, one per incident branch, and these are not 'disjoint' from one another. Points are the classes of non-disjointness. Segments are edges whose endpoints are such germs. The specialization relation (critical ⇒ non-critical) resembles the closure order of a stratification, or the order of a finite T0 / Alexandrov space: a vertex-germ specializes the generic lieu of its edge.

Under this reading the circle conjecture is coherent:
- A critical lieu is the germ of a single branch, so it bounds exactly one segment.
- Two distinct segments with the same boundary therefore need both endpoints non-critical.
- Each non-critical lieu has exactly two sides, so the component is a circle subdivided by two lieux.
- Multi-edges between two branch vertices (theta graph) cause no counterexample, because their endpoints are distinct germs.

The aim is a recursive, purely combinatorial category of 'tame' forms of each dimension. Dimension 1 is essentially graphs or 1-dimensional stratified spaces presented via germs at vertices, in a half-edge/flag formalism. This is the setting of dessins d'enfants and of the Esquisse's combinatorial models.
